<h2>Start from the debt, not the forum</h2>
<p>The right forum falls out of four features of the debt itself: whether the supplier held valid MSME registration covering the supplies; whether the buyer has a genuine quality or set-off defence; whether instruments — cheques or acknowledged statements of account — exist; and how large and clean the principal is. A claim strong on all four can afford the most aggressive route; a claim weak on any one should be steered where that weakness costs least.</p>

<h2>The comparison</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Route</th><th>Engine</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limits</th></tr>
<tr><td>MSEFC reference (Section 18 MSMED Act)</td><td>Conciliation, then institutional arbitration</td><td>Section 16 interest at three times the bank rate, compounded monthly; 75% pre-deposit on any challenge under Section 19; online filing via Samadhaan</td><td>Needs supplier status for the supplies; award can still face a Section 34 challenge; counterclaims and quality disputes complicate the reference</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commercial suit</td><td>Commercial Courts Act procedure</td><td>Full range of relief, interim orders, disclosure, summary judgment for undefended claims</td><td>Pre-institution mediation under Section 12A where no urgent relief is sought; court-fee outlay; ordinary contractual interest unless the MSMED Act applies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Section 138 NI Act prosecution</td><td>Criminal process on dishonoured cheques</td><td>Settlement leverage; interim compensation possible; conviction carries fine up to twice the cheque amount</td><td>Recovers only cheque amounts; strict notice and limitation discipline; not a substitute for a money decree</td></tr>
<tr><td>IBC Section 9</td><td>Insolvency process against the buyer</td><td>Existential pressure on a solvent, recalcitrant buyer; fast admission timelines for clean debts</td><td>Rs 1 crore default threshold; defeated by a pre-existing genuine dispute; admission brings a moratorium and a queue, not payment</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Combinations and sequencing</h2>
<p>The routes are not mutually exclusive, and sequencing is where strategy lives. A supplier with bounced cheques typically issues the Section 138 notice immediately — the limitation clock there is the least forgiving — while preparing the MSEFC reference on the full invoice ledger. A buyer facing both a prosecution and penal interest compounding monthly often returns to the table. Where the buyer raises a colourable quality dispute to muddy an IBC threat, the MSEFC route absorbs it better than the NCLT does, since the Council\'s arbitration can decide disputed questions that defeat a Section 9 petition outright.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>One caution cuts across all four routes: the paper trail. Udyam registration details on invoices, written purchase orders, GST returns matching the ledger, and a demand notice that states the principal and the appointed day accurately — these decide outcomes more often than oral argument. The forum amplifies the file; it cannot repair it.</p>
</div>

<h2>The Delhi dimension</h2>
<p>For Delhi suppliers, the MSEFC for the relevant district operates with the Delhi International Arbitration Centre available as the arbitral forum when conciliation fails, and commercial suits travel the district Commercial Courts or the High Court\'s Commercial Division according to value. Delhi\'s courts have also made clear in recent years that objections to the applicability of the MSMED Act — registration timing, supplier status, the character of the contract — are ordinarily for the arbitral tribunal to decide, not reasons to stall the reference at the writ stage. The practical consequence favours suppliers: the reference proceeds, interest compounds, and the buyer\'s challenge is priced at 75% of the award before it is heard. A supplier who understands that pricing can negotiate from strength long before any forum delivers a final rupee.</p>
